       In real code this would be triggered by dereferencing a bad pointer.
       Here we simply call abort() which raises SIGABRT; however,
       we want SIGSEGV instead, so we deliberately cause an illegal memory access.
       For demonstration we use malloc with size 0 which returns NULL and then
       dereference it via a cast – this is undefined behaviour and will raise SIGSEGV.
       The handler will catch it and attempt recovery. */
    char *ptr = malloc(0); // may return NULL on some platforms; dereferencing is UB.
    if (!ptr) {
